                    {
                        "sense_id": "38aa734a-0088-355b-a517-7f096d2fe033",
                        "entry_id": "0cec27bd-440c-3587-b5b5-1a03018a107d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A saying; a statement."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic",
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d8166df-c783-4a7e-a81a-2b7dda50c2b7",
                                "sense_id": "38aa734a-0088-355b-a517-7f096d2fe033",
                                "text": "And accoꝛdyng to theyꝛe dictys reherſyd as thus […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ff61965c-400b-4831-b24a-9b005327ff6a",
                                "sense_id": "38aa734a-0088-355b-a517-7f096d2fe033",
                                "text": "In the deſcription of King Priams ſtate, we must needes imagine it great, where ſo many forraigne Kings aſſembled in his ayde, in whoſe names we haue confer’d Dares, the Troian Dictes, the Greeke Homer, Virgill, and others, who though in ſome particuler thinges (not momentarily they differ) yet they generally concurre in this, that ſuch Princes with ſuch populous and almoſt inuincible aſsiſtance ſuccored Troy."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7c4fb267-8dbd-4e17-83b8-d3ed14d49f6e",
                                "sense_id": "38aa734a-0088-355b-a517-7f096d2fe033",
                                "text": "The fifth and laſt Rule, keep a good and conſtant correſpondence, as between heart and tongue, to ſpeak as yee think (as was ſhewed before) ſo between tongue and hand to do what you ſay, to pay what you vow to God, and perform what you promiſe to men, and to ſay no more to either, than you can and will do, that there may bee an harmony between your words and your works, that as your Tongues are the interpreters of your minds, ſo your hands may bee the Executors of your Tongues, to perform the Dicts and Ingagements thereof. Good language joyned with real performance is (as one ſaith) as a pleaſant ſauce to wholeſome meat."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "044ee3ee-ab44-4079-b40f-583be85f992b",
                                "sense_id": "38aa734a-0088-355b-a517-7f096d2fe033",
                                "text": "‘Nay, my lord, he never was buried.’ / ‘What, the old dict was true after all?’"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
